1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,362
Williams Obstetrics.

2
00:00:02,312 --> 00:00:05,650
Chapter 34: The Preterm Newborn.

3
00:00:05,650 --> 00:00:22,300
Respiratory Distress Syndrome Necrotizing Enterocolitis Retinopathy Of Prematurity Brain Disorders Intracranial Hemorrhage Cerebral Palsy References At the time of this textbook’s first edition in 1903, preterm delivery was often followed by neonatal death.

4
00:00:22,300 --> 00:00:30,612
However, subsequent scientific innovation has advanced the threshold of viability to 22 to 24 weeks’ gestation .

5
00:00:30,612 --> 00:00:38,462
Moreover, the overall survival rate for preterm neonates <29 weeks’ gestation approaches 80 percent .

6
00:00:38,462 --> 00:00:45,837
Still, the preterm newborn is susceptible to many serious medical complications both early and later in life .

7
00:00:45,837 --> 00:00:48,387
Table 34-1.

8
00:00:48,387 --> 00:00:57,600
Complications of Prematurity The complications of prematurity can be serious, and this is reflected in overall neonatal mortality rates.

9
00:00:57,600 --> 00:01:09,187
In 2018, two thirds of all infant deaths in the United States were in the 10 percent born before 37 weeks (Ely, 2020; Hamilton, 2021).

10
00:01:09,187 --> 00:01:17,562
Fortunately, rates of preterm birth have declined from approximately 12 percent in 2007 to 10 percent in 2020.

11
00:01:17,562 --> 00:01:29,787
The rate of early preterm births (<34 weeks’ gestation) declined from 2.77 to 2.69 percent, which is the lowest level reported since 2007 .

12
00:01:29,787 --> 00:01:32,375
Respiratory Distress Syndrome.

13
00:01:32,375 --> 00:01:38,425
The seminal complication of the preterm newborn is respiratory distress syndrome (RDS).

14
00:01:38,425 --> 00:01:45,650
This results from immature lungs that are unable to sustain necessary oxygenation due to surfactant deficiency.

15
00:01:45,650 --> 00:01:53,337
The resulting hypoxia can be associated with pulmonary hypertension and neurologic damage such as cerebral palsy.

16
00:01:53,337 --> 00:02:08,925
In addition, hyperoxia, a side effect of RDS treatment, contributes to other preterm morbidities including bronchopulmonary dysplasia, necrotizing enterocolitis, periventricular leukomalacia, and retinopathy of prematurity.

17
00:02:08,925 --> 00:02:11,862
# Etiopathogenesis.

18
00:02:11,862 --> 00:02:19,775
To provide blood gas exchange immediately following delivery, the lungs must rapidly fill with air while being cleared of fluid.

19
00:02:19,775 --> 00:02:24,262
Concurrently, pulmonary arterial blood flow must rise.

20
00:02:24,262 --> 00:02:37,500
Although some of the fluid is expelled as the chest is compressed during vaginal delivery, most is absorbed through the pulmonary lymphatics via complex mechanisms described in Chapter 32 (p. 586).

21
00:02:37,500 --> 00:02:45,162
Sufficient surfactant, synthesized by type II pneumocytes, is essential to stabilize the air-expanded alveoli.

22
00:02:45,162 --> 00:02:51,387
This lipoprotein lowers surface tension and thereby prevents lung collapse during expiration .

23
00:02:51,387 --> 00:02:59,050
If surfactant is inadequate, hyaline membranes form in the distal bronchioles and alveoli, and RDS develops.

24
00:02:59,050 --> 00:03:09,187
Although RDS is generally a disease of preterm neonates, it can develop in term newborns, especially with sepsis, pneumonia, or meconium aspiration.

25
00:03:09,187 --> 00:03:15,700
In these cases, surfactant can be inactivated by inflammation and/or the presence of meconium .

26
00:03:15,700 --> 00:03:22,650
With inadequate surfactant, alveoli are unstable and collapse with the low pressures of end expiration.

27
00:03:22,650 --> 00:03:28,012
Pneumocyte nutrition is compromised by hypoxia and systemic hypotension.

28
00:03:28,012 --> 00:03:35,162
Partial persistence of the fetal circulation may lead to pulmonary hypertension and a relative right-to-left shunt.

29
00:03:35,162 --> 00:03:39,587
Eventually, alveolar cells undergo ischemic necrosis.

30
00:03:39,587 --> 00:03:46,287
When oxygen therapy is initiated, the pulmonary vascular bed dilates, and the shunt reverses.

31
00:03:46,287 --> 00:03:52,050
Protein-filled fluid leaks into the alveolar ducts, and the cells lining the ducts slough.

32
00:03:52,050 --> 00:03:59,937
Hyaline membranes composed of fibrin-rich protein and cellular debris line the dilated alveoli and terminal bronchioles.

33
00:03:59,937 --> 00:04:04,137
The epithelium underlying the membrane becomes necrotic.

34
00:04:04,137 --> 00:04:14,150
At autopsy, with histological staining of lung tissue with hematoxylin-eosin, these membranes appear amorphous and eosinophilic, like hyaline cartilage.

35
00:04:14,150 --> 00:04:19,900
Because of this, respiratory distress syndrome is also termed hyaline membrane disease.

36
00:04:19,900 --> 00:04:22,375
# Clinical Course.

37
00:04:22,375 --> 00:04:36,150
In typical RDS, tachypnea develops, the chest wall retracts, and expiration is accompanied by nostril flaring and by grunting—in an attempt to provide a positive end-expiratory pressure to prevent lung collapse.

38
00:04:36,150 --> 00:04:43,525
Shunting of blood through nonventilated lung contributes to hypoxemia and to metabolic and respiratory acidosis.

39
00:04:43,525 --> 00:04:48,425
Poor peripheral circulation and systemic hypotension may be evident.

40
00:04:48,425 --> 00:04:50,125
The typical

41
00:04:50,284 --> 00:04:58,284
chest radiograph shows a diffuse reticulogranular infiltrate and an air-filled tracheobronchial tree—air bronchograms .

42
00:04:58,234 --> 00:05:00,834
Figure 34-1.

43
00:05:00,834 --> 00:05:06,209
Chest radiograph of a preterm newborn with moderate respiratory distress syndrome.

44
00:05:06,209 --> 00:05:11,946
Note the diffuse granularity throughout the lungs and mild, central, air bronchograms.

45
00:05:11,946 --> 00:05:16,759
The lungs are only eight ribs expanded despite mechanical ventilation.

46
00:05:16,759 --> 00:05:33,034
Respiratory insufficiency can also be caused by sepsis, pneumonia, meconium aspiration, pneumothorax, persistent fetal circulation, heart failure, and congenital malformations involving thoracic structures, such as diaphragmatic hernia .

47
00:05:33,034 --> 00:05:41,659
Common mutations in surfactant protein production and the phospholipid transporter (ABCA3) are rare causes of RDS .

48
00:05:41,659 --> 00:05:43,734
# Treatment.

49
00:05:43,734 --> 00:05:48,796
An important factor influencing survival is neonatal intensive care.

50
00:05:48,796 --> 00:05:59,221
Although hypoxemia prompts application of supplemental oxygen, excess oxygen can damage the pulmonary epithelium, retina, and other immature tissues.

51
00:05:59,221 --> 00:06:06,134
Despite this, advances in mechanical ventilation technology have improved neonatal survival rates.

52
00:06:06,134 --> 00:06:13,659
For example, continuous positive airway pressure (CPAP) prevents the collapse of unstable alveoli.

53
00:06:13,659 --> 00:06:20,646
This allows high concentrations of inspired oxygen to be reduced, thereby minimizing its toxicity.

54
00:06:20,646 --> 00:06:33,334
To help limit the need for tracheal intubation and intermittent positive-pressure ventilation, CPAP has been studied in well-designed trials (Morley, 2008; SUPPORT Study Group, 2010b).

55
00:06:33,334 --> 00:06:44,971
An initial CPAP strategy coupled with subsequent selective surfactant use is a beneficial alternative to immediate intubation and surfactant for neonates of extremely early gestational age .

56
00:06:44,971 --> 00:06:52,909
Less-invasive surfactant administration techniques allow surfactant delivery while maintaining the preterm newborn on CPAP.

57
00:06:52,909 --> 00:07:02,534
Such methods reduce the need for intubation and mechanical ventilation in the first 72 hours and decrease rates of death or bronchopulmonary dysplasia .

58
00:07:02,534 --> 00:07:14,346
Mechanical ventilation has undoubtedly improved survival rates, but it is an important factor in the genesis of chronic lung disease of prematurity— bronchopulmonary dysplasia (BPD).

59
00:07:14,346 --> 00:07:20,471
Namely, mechanical ventilation places a newborn at risk for barotrauma and volutrauma.

60
00:07:20,471 --> 00:07:26,534
Moreover, hyperoxia can create reactive oxygen species that trigger inflammation.

61
00:07:26,534 --> 00:07:29,559
Infection also can be contributory.

62
00:07:29,559 --> 00:07:39,846
In affected newborns, alveolar and pulmonary vascular development is disrupted, leading to hypoxia, hypercarbia, and chronic oxygen dependence.

63
00:07:39,846 --> 00:07:45,009
It can also affect adult lung function (Thébaud, 2019).

64
00:07:45,009 --> 00:07:50,146
BPD remains the most frequent complication of extreme preterm birth .

65
00:07:50,146 --> 00:07:57,684
High-frequency oscillatory ventilation is an alternative to conventional mechanical ventilation for preterm newborns.

66
00:07:57,684 --> 00:08:02,496
However, benefits and risks vary considerably between studies .

67
00:08:02,496 --> 00:08:11,209
Similarly, thus far, the evidence for high-frequency jet ventilation is not superior to conventional ventilation for this population .

68
00:08:11,209 --> 00:08:18,959
In the past, ventilator-dependent preterm neonates were often treated with glucocorticoids to prevent and treat BPD.

69
00:08:18,959 --> 00:08:23,634
This differs from antenatal use, described in the next sections.

70
00:08:23,634 --> 00:08:37,196
The American Academy of Pediatrics now recommends against routine neonatal corticosteroid use because of limited benefits and greater rates of impaired motor and cognitive function and school performance in exposed newborns .

71
00:08:37,196 --> 00:08:44,746
The risk of adverse neurodevelopment is highest if dexamethasone is given to preterm newborns at low risk of BPD.

72
00:08:44,746 --> 00:08:51,046
Attempts have been made to identify the highest-risk neonates for whom the benefits would exceed the risks .

73
00:08:51,046 --> 00:08:57,684
Different types of corticosteroids, with various timing and administration routes, have been studied.

74
00:08:57,684 --> 00:09:05,296
Current trials are focused on corticosteroids, mixed with surfactant, delivered intratracheally directly to the lung.

75
00:09:05,296 --> 00:09:10,321
This approach may avoid some systemic adverse effects of corticosteroids .

76
00:09:10,321 --> 00:09:19,859
In other efforts to prevent BPD, early animal studies demonstrated significant improvements in lung function with weeks of inhaled nitric oxide .

77
00:09:19,859 --> 00:09:26,096
Despite initial enthusiasm, clinical trials failed to demonstrate a consistent benefit .

78
00:09:26,096 --> 00:09:29,484
A National Institutes of Health (NIH)

79
00:09:29,644 --> 00:09:36,331
consensus statement concluded that the available data do not support its use to prevent or treat BPD .

80
00:09:36,281 --> 00:09:43,281
Caffeine has been used widely to treat apnea of prematurity, but it also has bronchodilatory effects.

81
00:09:43,281 --> 00:10:00,269
One large randomized trial of caffeine versus placebo showed lower BPD rates, improved neurodevelopmental outcomes during early childhood, and good evidence of safety up to 11 years (Schmidt, 2006, 2012, 2017).

82
00:10:00,269 --> 00:10:07,319
This therapy is now widely used for newborns weighing ≤1250 g.

83
00:10:07,319 --> 00:10:15,206
The antioxidant vitamin A is necessary for normal lung growth and the integrity of respiratory tract epithelial cells.

84
00:10:15,206 --> 00:10:22,919
Preterm newborns have low vitamin A levels at birth, and this is associated with a greater risk of developing BPD.

85
00:10:22,919 --> 00:10:32,831
Randomized trials support the use of vitamin A to modestly reduce BPD rates for very-low-birthweight neonates weighing <1500 g .

86
00:10:32,831 --> 00:10:39,794
Unfortunately, production shortages have limited the use of vitamin A prophylaxis in the past decade.

87
00:10:39,794 --> 00:10:56,994
Surfactant Prophylaxis and Rescue Therapy Surfactant replacement was established decades ago as an effective and safe therapy for RDS. Surfactant improves mortality rates, reduces the need for ventilation, and improves short-term outcomes for low-birth-weight neonates .

88
00:10:56,994 --> 00:11:00,419
It is standard therapy for newborn RDS .

89
00:11:00,419 --> 00:11:07,419
Exogenous surfactant products are most commonly delivered via endotracheal tube to help treat RDS.

90
00:11:07,419 --> 00:11:15,919
They contain biological or animal surfactants such as bovine—Survanta, calf—Infasurf, or porcine— Curosurf.

91
00:11:15,919 --> 00:11:25,456
One Cochrane review found that animal-derived surfactants led to better outcomes than synthetic ones, which do not contain important surfactant proteins .

92
00:11:25,456 --> 00:11:30,969
Currently, no synthetic surfactants are available, yet newer ones are in testing .

93
00:11:30,969 --> 00:11:39,231
For rescue surfactant therapy, less invasive methods of delivery to spontaneously breathing preterm neonates have been developed.

94
00:11:39,231 --> 00:11:47,319
These include surfactant application into the pharynx, surfactant nebulization, and application via laryngeal mask .

95
00:11:47,319 --> 00:12:07,844
Administration of surfactant via a thin catheter while maintaining the newborn on CPAP is associated with reduced risk of death or BPD, lower intubation rate in the first 72 hours, reduced incidence of major complications, and a reduced inhospital mortality rate compared with administration via an endotracheal tube .

96
00:12:07,844 --> 00:12:13,031
Surfactant has been used for prophylaxis of preterm, at-risk newborns.

97
00:12:13,031 --> 00:12:21,331
Given together, antenatal corticosteroids and postnatal surfactant result in an even greater reduction in the overall death rate.

98
00:12:21,331 --> 00:12:41,956
However, randomized trials indicate that in populations with high use of antenatal corticosteroids and routine use of CPAP in the delivery room, prophylactic surfactant is no longer beneficial and is associated with greater risk of death or BPD (RojasReyes, 2012; Sardesai, 2017).

99
00:12:41,956 --> 00:12:44,106
# Prevention.

100
00:12:44,106 --> 00:13:02,931
Antenatal Corticosteroids More than two decades ago, the NIH (2000) concluded that a single course of antenatal corticosteroid therapy reduces RDS and intraventricular hemorrhage rates in preterm neonates born between 24 and 34 weeks’ gestation (p. 619).

101
00:13:02,931 --> 00:13:14,994
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2020a) considers all women at risk for preterm birth in this gestational-age range to be potential candidates for therapy.

102
00:13:14,994 --> 00:13:23,356
It also may be considered for pregnant women starting at 23 weeks’ gestation who are at risk of preterm delivery within 7 days.

103
00:13:23,356 --> 00:13:31,356
For the periviable fetus, other management guidelines are listed in Table 45-4 (p. 786).

104
00:13:31,356 --> 00:13:44,944
Administration of corticosteroids for pregnant women during the periviable period who are at risk of preterm delivery within 7 days should be linked to a family’s decision regarding resuscitation and should be considered in that context.

105
00:13:44,944 --> 00:13:52,394
Thus, as survival at even earlier gestations improves, the threshold for administration may decrease as well.

106
00:13:52,394 --> 00:14:04,481
Administration of antenatal corticosteroids to women at risk for latepreterm delivery (34 to 36 weeks’ gestation) reduces the rate of neonatal respiratory complications .

107
00:14:04,481 --> 00:14:12,731
Consideration of a single course of betamethasone for women at risk for late-preterm delivery is supported by the American College of

108
00:14:12,892 --> 00:14:17,079
Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2020a).

109
00:14:17,029 --> 00:14:25,054
Based on data presented in Chapter 45 (p. 802), at Parkland Hospital, this is not currently our practice.

110
00:14:25,054 --> 00:14:35,192
Amniocentesis to Assess Fetal Lung Maturity Amniocentesis to assess fetal lung maturity was used previously to predict the neonatal risk for RDS.

111
00:14:35,192 --> 00:14:49,767
However, this use of amniocentesis to guide delivery decisions in any clinical situation of pregnancy is not currently supported or recommended (American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, 2021a,b).

112
00:14:49,767 --> 00:14:52,579
Necrotizing Enterocolitis.

113
00:14:52,579 --> 00:15:02,142
This newborn bowel disorder has clinical findings that include abdominal distention, emesis, ileus, bilious gastric aspirates, and bloody stools.

114
00:15:02,142 --> 00:15:10,367
Often, radiological images show pneumatosis intestinalis, which is bowel wall gas derived from invading bacteria .

115
00:15:10,367 --> 00:15:16,042
Other classic imaging findings include hepatobiliary gas and pneumoperitoneum.

116
00:15:16,042 --> 00:15:19,779
Bowel perforation necessitates prompt resection.

117
00:15:19,779 --> 00:15:22,304
Figure 34-2.

118
00:15:22,304 --> 00:15:27,704
Abdominal radiograph of a preterm newborn with necrotizing enterocolitis.

119
00:15:27,704 --> 00:15:31,804
Note the distended air-filled loops of large and small bowel.

120
00:15:31,804 --> 00:15:40,629
Significant pneumatosis intestinalis, which is intramural bowel gas, is visualized within the right lower quadrant and within the pelvis.

121
00:15:40,629 --> 00:15:45,879
Not seen here, an associated finding is air in the portal veins of the liver.

122
00:15:45,879 --> 00:15:54,204
Necrotizing enterocolitis (NEC) is seen primarily in low-birthweight newborns but occasionally in mature neonates.

123
00:15:54,204 --> 00:16:07,979
Hypothesized causes include perinatal hypotension, hypoxia, sepsis, umbilical catheterization, exchange transfusions, blood transfusions, and the feeding of cow milk and hypertonic solutions .

124
00:16:07,979 --> 00:16:29,967
The pathophysiology is thought to be multifactorial, and genetic disposition, intestinal immaturity, imbalance in microvascular tone, abnormal microbial colonization in the intestine, exposure to enteral feeds, and highly immunoreactive intestinal mucosa play potential roles (Bazacliu, 2019; Neu, 2011).

125
00:16:29,967 --> 00:16:37,992
Medical treatment includes abdominal decompression, bowel rest, broad-spectrum antibiotics, and parenteral nutrition.

126
00:16:37,992 --> 00:16:45,517
Surgery is reserved for neonates with intestinal perforation or deteriorating clinical or biochemical status.

127
00:16:45,517 --> 00:16:55,367
Possible surgical procedures include drain placement, exploratory laparotomy with resection of diseased bowel, or enterostomy with creation of a stoma .

128
00:16:55,367 --> 00:16:58,129
Retinopathy Of Prematurity.

129
00:16:58,129 --> 00:17:07,079
By 1950, this condition, formerly known as retrolental fibroplasia, became the largest single cause of blindness in this country.

130
00:17:07,079 --> 00:17:18,029
After the discovery that the disease resulted from hyperoxemia, its frequency declined but began to rise again with the increasing survival rates of extremely preterm newborns.

131
00:17:18,029 --> 00:17:28,379
Normally, the fetal retina vascularizes centrifugally from the optic nerve starting at approximately the fourth month, and this continues until shortly after birth.

132
00:17:28,379 --> 00:17:38,017
During vascularization, excessive oxygen induces severe retinal vasoconstriction, which leads to endothelial damage and vessel obliteration.

133
00:17:38,017 --> 00:17:46,504
This is followed by subsequent aberrant neovascularization, in which the new vessels penetrate the retina and extend into the vitreous.

134
00:17:46,504 --> 00:17:50,729
Here, they are prone to leak proteins or to burst and bleed.

135
00:17:50,729 --> 00:17:53,992
Adhesions can form to detach the retina.

136
00:17:53,992 --> 00:18:07,117
Vascular endothelial growth factor (VEGF) contributes to normal angiogenesis and is upregulated during retinopathy of prematurity (ROP) development (Hellström, 2019).

137
00:18:07,117 --> 00:18:12,579
This understanding has opened new avenues of treatment with anti-VEGF therapies.

138
00:18:12,579 --> 00:18:18,692
The precise levels of hyperoxemia that can be sustained without causing ROP are unknown.

139
00:18:18,692 --> 00:18:29,117
After birth, there is a “relative” hyperoxia compared with in-utero oxygen content, even in newborns not exposed to higher inspired oxygen concentrations.

140
00:18:29,117 --> 00:18:47,129
To better understand the oxygen saturation threshold necessary to minimize ROP without raising rates of other adverse outcomes, the Neonatal Research Network performed a randomized trial of oxygenation in 1316 neonates born between 24 and 27 weeks’ gestation .

141
00:18:47,129 --> 00:18:56,267
The two target ranges of oxygen saturation were 85 to 89 percent in one arm and 91 to 95 percent in the other arm.

142
00:18:56,267 --> 00:19:00,792
These targets were both commonly employed in neonatal intensive

143
00:19:00,940 --> 00:19:02,715
care units.

144
00:19:02,665 --> 00:19:10,852
Death before discharge occurred significantly more frequently in the lower-oxygen saturation group—20 versus 16 percent.

145
00:19:10,852 --> 00:19:21,702
However, severe ROP among survivors developed significantly less often in the lower-oxygen saturation group—8.6 versus 17.9 percent.

146
00:19:21,702 --> 00:19:23,627
Brain Disorders.

147
00:19:23,627 --> 00:19:31,740
Central nervous system injury usually creates different neuroanatomical sequelae in preterm newborns compared with those at term .

148
00:19:31,740 --> 00:19:47,177
In preterm neonates, cerebral lesions detected by neuroimaging include intraventricular hemorrhage, cerebellar hemorrhage, periventricular hemorrhagic infarction, cystic periventricular leukomalacia, and diffuse white matter injury.

149
00:19:47,177 --> 00:19:52,240
All of these are strongly associated with adverse neurodevelopmental outcomes .

150
00:19:52,240 --> 00:20:01,102
Cranial sonography remains the preferred approach for detecting frequently occurring brain abnormalities and acute events in the preterm neonate.

151
00:20:01,102 --> 00:20:07,765
It is readily available and reliable for detecting common abnormalities and monitoring brain growth.

152
00:20:07,765 --> 00:20:14,677
Because cystic injuries may take 2 to 5 weeks to evolve, serial scans are obtained during this time.

153
00:20:14,677 --> 00:20:23,902
In those whose findings are transient and resolve in the neonatal period, prognosis is improved compared with infants whose lesions remain and evolve.

154
00:20:23,902 --> 00:20:33,865
At the same time, however, between 4 and 10 percent of prematurely born children may develop cerebral palsy (CP) in the absence of lesions.

155
00:20:33,865 --> 00:20:43,052
Put another way, 90 to 96 percent of preterm newborns with CP have cerebral lesions that are detectable using cranial sonography.

156
00:20:43,052 --> 00:20:45,927
# Intracranial Hemorrhage.

157
00:20:45,927 --> 00:20:54,052
There are five major categories of intracranial hemorrhage in the neonate (Table 33-4, p. 607) .

158
00:20:54,052 --> 00:21:00,052
Primary subarachnoid hemorrhage is more common in those born preterm and is frequently benign.

159
00:21:00,052 --> 00:21:08,002
Cerebellar hemorrhage is also more frequent in preterm neonates and is increasingly recognized as a cause of serious sequelae.

160
00:21:08,002 --> 00:21:20,477
Intraventricular hemorrhage (IVH) is almost exclusively seen in preterm newborns, is relatively common, and can have serious effects (Hintz, 2015, 2018).

161
00:21:20,477 --> 00:21:25,690
Subdural hemorrhages are more frequent in term newborns and can be serious .

162
00:21:25,690 --> 00:21:32,465
Miscellaneous intraparenchymal hemorrhage is also more frequent in those born at term and is of variable concern.

163
00:21:32,465 --> 00:21:35,477
# Intraventricular Hemorrhage.

164
00:21:35,477 --> 00:21:43,127
The germinal matrix is a highly cellular and vascular region of the developing brain located beneath the lateral ventricles.

165
00:21:43,127 --> 00:21:49,515
In preterm neonates, the germinal matrix capillary network is fragile for several reasons.

166
00:21:49,515 --> 00:21:55,752
First, the subependymal germinal matrix provides poor support for the vessels coursing through it.

167
00:21:55,752 --> 00:22:05,227
Second, venous anatomy in this region causes stasis and congestion, which makes vessels susceptible to bursting if intravascular pressure rises.

168
00:22:05,227 --> 00:22:14,302
Third, vascular autoregulation is impaired in the preterm neonate (Verhagen, 2014; Volpe, 2016).

169
00:22:14,302 --> 00:22:22,965
If fragile capillaries in the germinal matrix rupture, blood escapes into the ventricular system—intraventricular hemorrhage (IVH).

170
00:22:22,965 --> 00:22:28,865
This may dilate the ventricle, which then can obstruct venous drainage of the surrounding tissue.

171
00:22:28,865 --> 00:22:36,190
As a result, venous infarction and hemorrhage, termed periventricular hemorrhagic infarction, may follow.

172
00:22:36,190 --> 00:22:40,990
Risk of severe IVH is inversely related to gestational age .

173
00:22:40,990 --> 00:22:50,402
IVH is a serious complication of preterm neonates born before 32 weeks and is particularly frequent in those born before 27 weeks.

174
00:22:50,402 --> 00:22:55,877
The germinal matrix involutes between 34 and 36 weeks’ gestation.

175
00:22:55,877 --> 00:23:00,740
Thus, IVH is infrequent after that period of brain development .

176
00:23:00,740 --> 00:23:04,902
Most hemorrhages develop within 72 hours of birth .

177
00:23:04,902 --> 00:23:13,627
Because IVH usually is recognized within 3 days of delivery, its genesis is sometimes erroneously attributed to birth events.

178
00:23:13,627 --> 00:23:18,252
It is important to realize that prelabor IVH also can occur .

179
00:23:18,252 --> 00:23:36,315
The pathogenesis of IVH is multifactorial and includes hypoxic-ischemic events, carbon dioxide elevations, anatomical factors, blood pressure instability, coagulopathy, genetic factors, and many others (Gilard, 2020; Leijser, 2019).

180
00:23:36,315 --> 00:23:46,877
Moreover, preterm birth is frequently associated with infection, which further predisposes to endothelial activation, platelet adherence, and thrombi .

181
00:23:46,877 --> 00:23:49,327
Respiratory distress and

182
00:23:49,492 --> 00:23:53,779
mechanical ventilation are commonly associated factors .

183
00:23:53,729 --> 00:23:57,267
Almost half of hemorrhages are clinically silent.

184
00:23:57,267 --> 00:24:04,242
Most small germinal matrix hemorrhages and those confined to the cerebral ventricles resolve without impairment.

185
00:24:04,242 --> 00:24:08,917
But, nearly half do show some sign of neurological impairment .

186
00:24:08,917 --> 00:24:16,742
Survivors of extensive periventricular/intraventricular hemorrhage can have major neurodevelopmental handicaps .

187
00:24:16,742 --> 00:24:26,342
Large lesions can result in hydrocephalus or in degenerated cystic areas termed periventricular leukomalacia (PVL), discussed later.

188
00:24:26,342 --> 00:24:30,954
Importantly, the extent of PVL correlates with CP risk .

189
00:24:30,954 --> 00:24:36,754
Incidence and Severity IVH incidences vary according to gestational age at birth.

190
00:24:36,754 --> 00:24:43,179
Severe IVH rates have declined (Handley, 2018; Yeo, 2020).

191
00:24:43,179 --> 00:24:54,204
From the Neonatal Research Network, the incidence from 1993 to 2012 for neonates <29 weeks’ gestation dropped from 19 to 13 percent .

192
00:24:54,204 --> 00:25:03,254
In 2012, the incidence ranged from 11 percent in those born at 23 weeks to only 5 percent for those born at 28 weeks.

193
00:25:03,254 --> 00:25:07,854
The severity of IVH can be assessed by neuroimaging studies.

194
00:25:07,854 --> 00:25:17,229
Papile and coworkers (1978) devised the most widely used grading scheme to quantify the lesion extent and estimate prognosis.

195
00:25:17,229 --> 00:25:44,392
Since the initial report, the Papile classification has been modified : • Grade I—hemorrhage is limited to the germinal matrix • Grade II—IVH occupies 10 to 50 percent of the ventricular area • Grade III—hemorrhage with >50 percent of the ventricular area involved • Parenchymal hemorrhage (previously called Grade IV)—most likely attributable to hemorrhagic venous infarction rather than extension of IVH into the parenchyma.

196
00:25:44,392 --> 00:25:53,854
Antenatal Corticosteroids If given at least 48 hours before delivery, corticosteroids prevent or reduce the incidence of severe IVH .

197
00:25:53,854 --> 00:26:08,329
A Consensus Development Conference of the NIH (1994) concluded that such therapy reduced rates of mortality, RDS, and IVH in preterm neonates born between 24 and 32 weeks’ gestation.

198
00:26:08,329 --> 00:26:16,654
A second consensus statement by the NIH (2000) recommended that repeated courses of corticosteroids not be given .

199
00:26:16,654 --> 00:26:31,554
Subsequently, the Maternal–Fetal Medicine Units Network reported that repeated corticosteroid courses were associated with some improved preterm neonatal outcomes, but also with reduced birthweight and increased risk for fetal-growth restriction .

200
00:26:31,554 --> 00:26:43,729
Surveillance of this cohort through age 2 to 3 years found that children exposed to repeated versus single-dose corticosteroid courses did not differ significantly in physical or neurocognitive measures .

201
00:26:43,729 --> 00:26:52,642
It was worrisome, however, that infants exposed to multiple courses had a nonsignificant 5.7-fold relative risk of CP.

202
00:26:52,642 --> 00:27:01,067
At the same time, the 2-year follow-up of the Australian Collaborative Trial was reported by Crowther and coworkers (2007).

203
00:27:01,067 --> 00:27:13,817
In more than 1100 newborns, the incidence of CP was almost identical—4.2 versus 4.8 percent —in those given repeated versus single-course corticosteroids, respectively.

204
00:27:13,817 --> 00:27:26,067
More recently, it was reported that for those born before 28 weeks’ gestation, if 10 days or more had passed since betamethasone administration, the incidence of severe IVH was higher .

205
00:27:26,067 --> 00:27:29,617
This is not a consistent finding in all studies .

206
00:27:29,617 --> 00:27:46,967
The most recent recommendations from the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2020a) are for a single course of corticosteroids for pregnant women between 240/7 weeks and 336/7 weeks’ gestation who are at risk for preterm delivery.

207
00:27:46,967 --> 00:27:56,342
They further note that those given their initial course more than 14 days prior and who have imminent risk of preterm delivery may receive a second “rescue” course.

208
00:27:56,342 --> 00:28:08,317
Other Preventive Methods Although antenatal magnesium sulfate for those at risk for preterm delivery does not reduce the incidence of IVH, it does offer protection from neurodevelopmental impairment .

209
00:28:08,317 --> 00:28:20,079
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2020b) recommends its use for this indication, as discussed further in Chapter 45 (p. 803).

210
00:28:20,079 --> 00:28:33,104
Antenatal vitamin K and phenobarbital and postnatal phenobarbital have not consistently reduced the incidence of IVH in studies (Crowther, 2010a,b; Smit, 2013).

211
00:28:33,268 --> 00:28:39,443
Although vitamin E reduced IVH rates, the associated risk for sepsis was increased .

212
00:28:39,393 --> 00:28:53,968
Metaanalyses of the many randomized trials of postnatal indomethacin and other prostaglandin inhibitors show inconsistent results regarding reductions in IVH rates (Fowlie, 2010; Mitra, 2018).

213
00:28:53,968 --> 00:28:58,093
None has shown improvements in neurodevelopmental impairment.

214
00:28:58,093 --> 00:29:05,068
The benefits of cesarean delivery compared with vaginal birth to lower IVH rates remains controversial.

215
00:29:05,068 --> 00:29:15,280
One metaanalysis reported that cesarean delivery for very-low-birthweight neonates had no effect on rates of severe IVH but did reduce overall IVH rates .

216
00:29:15,280 --> 00:29:24,093
The benefits of cesarean delivery to lower IVH rates in breech and growth-restricted fetuses are unclear from current observational data .

217
00:29:24,093 --> 00:29:26,580
Prospective trials are needed.

218
00:29:26,580 --> 00:29:42,118
Delayed umbilical cord clamping compared to immediate cord clamping has been reported to reduce the risk for any grade of IVH in preterm newborns but has not reduced rates of severe IVH (Rabe, 2019; Seidler, 2021).

219
00:29:42,118 --> 00:29:50,280
In preterm neonates, deferred cord clamping may also raise hemoglobin and hematocrit levels and improve survival rates .

220
00:29:50,280 --> 00:30:02,230
Cord milking is associated with higher rates of severe IVH in neonates <28 weeks’ gestation and should be avoided (Katheria, 2019; Wyckoff, 2020).

221
00:30:02,230 --> 00:30:05,593
# Periventricular Leukomalacia.

222
00:30:05,593 --> 00:30:13,880
This histological description refers to cystic areas deep in brain white matter that develop after hemorrhagic or ischemic infarction.

223
00:30:13,880 --> 00:30:17,393
Tissue ischemia leads to regional necrosis.

224
00:30:17,393 --> 00:30:28,443
Because brain tissue does not regenerate and the preterm neonate has minimal gliosis, these irreversibly damaged areas appear as echolucent cysts in neuroimaging studies.

225
00:30:28,443 --> 00:30:35,618
Generally, they require at least 2 weeks to form but may develop as late as 4 months after the initial insult.

226
00:30:35,618 --> 00:30:40,993
Thus, their presence at birth may help to determine the timing of an inciting event.

227
00:30:40,993 --> 00:30:43,105
Cerebral Palsy.

228
00:30:43,105 --> 00:30:53,055
This group of conditions is characterized by chronic movement or posture abnormalities that are cerebral in origin, arise early in life, and are nonprogressive .

229
00:30:53,055 --> 00:30:57,618
Epilepsy and mental retardation frequently accompany CP.

230
00:30:57,618 --> 00:31:05,380
Its cause differs in preterm and term neonates, and the latter is described in Chapter 33 (p. 602).

231
00:31:05,380 --> 00:31:19,668
CP is commonly classified by the type of neurological dysfunction— spastic, dyskinetic, or ataxic—and by the number and distribution of limbs involved—quadriplegia, diplegia, hemiplegia, or monoplegia.

232
00:31:19,668 --> 00:31:26,143
Spastic quadriplegia has a strong association with developmental retardation and epilepsy.

233
00:31:26,143 --> 00:31:34,205
Spastic quadriplegia is the most severe form of spastic CP and affects all four limbs, the trunk, and the face.

234
00:31:34,205 --> 00:31:39,030
Spastic diplegia is common in preterm or low-birthweight neonates.

235
00:31:39,030 --> 00:31:45,568
In this CP type, muscle stiffness is mainly in the legs, and the arms are less or not involved.

236
00:31:45,568 --> 00:31:54,680
Affected persons might have difficulty walking because tight extremity muscles cause their legs to pull together, turn inward, and cross at the knees.

237
00:31:54,680 --> 00:32:02,068
Spastic hemiplegia affects only one side of a person’s body, and the arm is affected usually more than the leg.

238
00:32:02,068 --> 00:32:08,630
Dyskinetic CP includes athetoid, choreoathetoid, and dystonic cerebral forms.

239
00:32:08,630 --> 00:32:14,643
Poor control of hand, arm, feet, and leg movements make sitting and walking difficult.

240
00:32:14,643 --> 00:32:18,693
Movements may be slow and writhing or rapid and jerky.

241
00:32:18,693 --> 00:32:23,580
Ataxic CP manifests as problems with balance and coordination.

242
00:32:23,580 --> 00:32:28,305
Mixed CP manifests with symptoms of more than one CP type.

243
00:32:28,305 --> 00:32:32,293
The most common type is spastic–dyskinetic CP .

244
00:32:32,293 --> 00:32:34,455
# Incidence.

245
00:32:34,455 --> 00:32:45,030
According to the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (2020a), the prevalence of CP in the United States approximates 3 cases in 1000 children.

246
00:32:45,030 --> 00:32:56,705
Hafström and coworkers (2018) reported the 6-year follow-up of all babies born at gestational ages <28 weeks in Sweden from 2004 to 2007.

247
00:32:56,705 --> 00:33:06,605
The CP rate overall was 10.5 percent, and no linear association was found between gestational age at birth and CP rates in this cohort.

248
00:33:06,605 --> 00:33:08,680
# Risks.

249
00:33:08,680 --> 00:33:18,993
Intraventricular Hemorrhage Various clinical and pathological data link CP with associated severe IVH (grade III or IV) and resulting PVL.

250
00:33:18,993 --> 00:33:24,468
Low-grade IVH is generally considered benign, but it is associated with higher

251
00:33:24,628 --> 00:33:29,103
rates of CP in school-aged children born extremely preterm .

252
00:33:29,053 --> 00:33:33,428
Higher IVH grades are associated with even greater rates.

253
00:33:33,428 --> 00:33:38,665
Ischemia Preterm newborns are susceptible to brain ischemia and PVL.

254
00:33:38,665 --> 00:33:47,028
This is in part due to immature cerebral vasculature and an immature cerebrovascular autoregulation system that is pressure passive.

255
00:33:47,028 --> 00:33:58,328
In response to systemic hypotension, preterm infants cannot increase cerebral perfusion to borderzone regions of the brain supplied by the immature penetrating cerebral vasculature .

256
00:33:58,328 --> 00:34:07,415
This triggers a cascade of events caused by hypoxia–ischemia that results in pyramidal tract damage, which can cause spastic diplegia.

257
00:34:07,415 --> 00:34:13,840
Perinatal Infection/Inflammation PVL is associated with infection and inflammation.

258
00:34:13,840 --> 00:34:22,703
In one study of 753 neonates born between 24 and 32 weeks’ gestation, 9 percent developed PVL .

259
00:34:22,703 --> 00:34:32,378
Those born before 28 weeks, those who had inflammatory events during the last days to weeks before delivery, and those who had both were at highest risk.

260
00:34:32,378 --> 00:34:41,290
In another study, PVL was strongly associated with prolonged membrane rupture, chorioamnionitis, and neonatal hypotension .

261
00:34:41,290 --> 00:34:50,053
Bailis and coworkers (2008) reported that chronic—and not acute—placental inflammation was associated with PVL.

262
00:34:50,053 --> 00:35:00,065
In another study of 3094 singletons born before 33 weeks’ gestation, 15 percent had evidence of clinical chorioamnionitis .

263
00:35:00,065 --> 00:35:09,553
Compared with noninfected infants, cases complicated by infection had significantly higher rates of IVH—22 versus 12 percent.

264
00:35:09,553 --> 00:35:20,940
Fetal infection may be a key element in the pathway between preterm birth and CP (Burd, 2012; Leviton, 2010, Novack, 2018).

265
00:35:20,940 --> 00:35:29,915
As discussed in Chapter 45 (p. 789), chorioamnionitis is a major cause of spontaneous preterm delivery.

266
00:35:29,915 --> 00:35:43,753
In the pathway proposed in Figure 34-3, antenatal reproductive tract infection evokes the production of cytokines such as tumor necrosis factor (TNF) and interleukins 1, 6, and 8.

267
00:35:43,753 --> 00:35:48,678
These in turn stimulate prostaglandin production and preterm labor.

268
00:35:48,678 --> 00:36:00,990
Preterm fetal intracranial blood vessels are susceptible to rupture and damage, and the cytokines that stimulate preterm labor also have direct toxic effects on oligodendrocytes and myelin.

269
00:36:00,990 --> 00:36:08,165
Vessel rupture, tissue hypoxia, and cytokine-mediated damage result in massive neuronal cell death.

270
00:36:08,165 --> 00:36:15,103
Glutamate is released, which stimulates membrane receptors to allow excess calcium to enter the neurons.

271
00:36:15,103 --> 00:36:23,140
High intracellular calcium levels are toxic to white matter, and glutamate may be directly toxic to oligodendrocytes .

272
00:36:23,140 --> 00:36:42,540
Inflammation induced by the activation of microglia and macrophages with the production and release of proinflammatory cytokines, chemokines, proteases, complement factors, excitotoxic amino acids, reactive oxygen species, and nitric oxide further exacerbates secondary brain injury .

273
00:36:42,540 --> 00:36:45,178
Figure 34-3.

274
00:36:45,178 --> 00:36:54,840
Schematic representation of the hypothesized pathway between maternal or intrauterine infection and preterm birth or periventricular leukomalacia.

275
00:36:54,840 --> 00:36:58,140
Both potentially lead to cerebral palsy.

276
00:36:58,140 --> 00:37:03,565
LPS = lipopolysaccharide; PG = prostaglandin.

277
00:37:03,565 --> 00:37:13,390
Many studies show that infection and cytokines can directly damage the immature brain (Chau, 2014; Yoon, 1997a).

278
00:37:13,390 --> 00:37:19,828
TNF and interleukin 6 were more frequently found in the brains of infants who died with PVL .

279
00:37:19,828 --> 00:37:26,103
Cytokines are strongly linked to white matter lesions even when organisms cannot be demonstrated .

280
00:37:26,103 --> 00:37:29,290
# Prevention—Neuroprotection.

281
00:37:29,290 --> 00:37:35,028
The benefits of antenatal magnesium sulfate and corticosteroids are well described .

282
00:37:35,028 --> 00:37:44,153
Another potential neuroprotective therapy is erythropoiesis-stimulating agents (ESAs) such as erythropoietin and darbepoetin.

283
00:37:44,153 --> 00:37:50,928
In addition to stimulating erythropoiesis, ESAs protect the developing brain in animal models .

284
00:37:50,928 --> 00:37:59,128
One recent metaanalysis found decreased rates of IVH and PVL in preterm neonates treated with ESAs soon after birth.

285
00:37:59,128 --> 00:38:03,103
The effects on neurodevelopmental impairment are unclear .

286
00:38:03,103 --> 00:38:13,478
In one large trial, early high-dose erythropoietin compared with placebo did not lower the risk of severe neurodevelopmental impairment or death at age 2 years .

287
00:38:13,478 --> 00:38:19,890
A large trial of the longeracting darbepoetin has completed enrollment and is in the follow-up phase

288
00:38:20,044 --> 00:38:28,144
clinicaltrials.gov/ct2/show/NCT01471015).

289
00:38:28,094 --> 00:38:40,269
Last, discussed in Chapter 33 (p. 602) for neonates >36 weeks, induced hypothermia for neuroprotection is not recommended for preterm newborns.

290
00:38:40,269 --> 00:38:47,056
This stems from their different pattern of gray and white matter injury and their greater susceptibility to cold stress.

291
00:38:47,056 --> 00:38:56,944
The exact gestational age for which therapeutic cooling for hypoxicischemic encephalopathy (HIE) would provide more benefit than harm is unknown.

292
00:38:56,944 --> 00:39:08,181
A trial of cooling for neonates born at 330/7 to 356/7 weeks’ gestation with moderate/severe HIE has completed enrollment and is in the follow-up phase.

